Durarara!! AU
Chapter 28 – Part 1
Morning class looked like a normal Monday if you did not know about tea and vans.
Curtis came in first. Uniform. Braids. The face of a man who had already lived a day. He dropped into his seat like it owed him sleep.
Mikado arrived two minutes later. Hair a little too neat. Eyes a little too awake.
Curtis tipped his chin.
“Morning, Mikado.”
“Morning.”
Masaomi’s gaze bounced between them like a pinball that had found a new bumper.
“You two buddies?”
Curtis smiled without lifting his head all the way.
“The best.”
He settled. Arms folded. Face down. The performance of a transfer student who missed trains.
Mikado sat, opened a notebook, and did not look at Masaomi when he said it.
“Don’t be fooled. He may sleep, but he’s also always listening.”
Masaomi blinked.
“…That’s the creepiest endorsement I’ve ever heard before homeroom.”
Anri’s pencil paused. Rio’s eyes flicked from Curtis’s nape to Mikado’s mouth. Neither of them said Mika. The room did not deserve that word.
Mikado kept his brightness down. On purpose.
Curtis, cheek on his sleeve, listened anyway — chairs, Masaomi’s laugh starting and dying, Anri’s breath when she glanced over, the clock.
Onboard. DL. Harmless.
On a Raira morning, friendship put on a costume.
Masaomi Kida smelled a new alliance
and did not know it was a lock.
Curtis Nightstar slept with his ears open.
Mikado Ryuugamine told the truth
in the only way class would allow.
The lunch bell saved nobody.
Curtis listed sideways out of his desk like a building with one bolt left. Chair scraped. Shoulder hit the floor. A few people clapped because high school — college — never grew out of that.
He stayed there a second, stared at the ceiling tiles, then laughed with the room.
“Two hours of sleep is catching up. Oh dear.”
Anri was already half out of her seat. “Curtis-kun—”
“I’m good.” He rolled to a sit, braids a mess, grin crooked. “Gravity wins. Lunch wins. I lose.”
Rio’s mouth twitched. “You look like you fought the night and the night invoiced you.”
“Something like that.”
Masaomi leaned on Mikado’s desk, delighted.
“Best buddies and he can’t even stay in the chair. What did you two do, secret club at dawn?”
Mikado’s laugh came out too thin.
“He sleeps in class. I told you. Sometimes the class ends and he keeps going.”
Curtis stood, dusted his uniform, and pointed vaguely at the door.
“Food. Then I will try not to die in the hallway. Don’t write a report.”
He meant it as a joke. Anri still watched him like the floor might take a second try. Mikado watched him like a door that had learned to walk. Masaomi watched both of them like a man who could smell a story and had not been invited.
On a Raira lunch break, two hours of night
fell out of a desk.
Curtis Nightstar laughed so the room would stay ordinary.
The ordinary was the point.
Mikado waited until the classroom thinned.
“There’s a girl,” he said, like the words might bite him. “At my place. She’s… staying. For a bit. It’s not— don’t make that face.”
Masaomi made the face anyway and nudged him hard enough to rock the desk.
“Mikado. You dog!!”
“It’s not like that.”
“It’s exactly like that. First year in the city, own room, mystery girl. I’m proud. I’m also going to need details that won’t get me punched.”
Mikado’s ears went red. The reconstructed neck stayed behind his teeth.
“Do you know anyone in the Dollars.”
The pride died on Masaomi’s mouth. He grimaced like the color tasted old.
“…Why.”
“Because I need to know who talks. Who pretends. Who would knock on a door if a rumor moved.”
Masaomi scratched his cheek, looked at the window, looked anywhere but Ikebukuro’s real map.
“There’s a boy on the roof who claims he is one. Loud about it. Yellow-adjacent attitude, Dollars mouth. I wouldn’t take him shopping. I also wouldn’t tell him you have a guest.” A thinner look. “Kida advice: don’t go collecting colors because a girl slept on your floor. That’s how people stop being dorks.”
Mikado nodded like he had heard the warning and filed the roof anyway.

Down the hall, Curtis did not get a choice of direction.
Rio had one sleeve. Anri had the other. He let both of them have him because standing upright was already a job.
“Lunch,” Anri said.
“And water,” Rio added. “You fell out of a desk.”
“Two hours,” Curtis muttered, amused and ruined. “I’m a tourist. Be gentle.”
They were not gentle. They steered. Students parted around a tall foreigner being towed by two girls who did not look at each other if they could help it.
Anri’s voice stayed soft. “You can sleep after you eat.”
Rio’s stayed dryer. “He can sleep when he stops volunteering for other people’s nights.”
Curtis laughed once. It cost him.
“Yes, bosses.”
On two sides of the same lunch hour, a girl became a rumor
and a rumor became a roof.
Masaomi Kida thought Mikado had gotten lucky.
Mikado Ryuugamine thought about Dollars mouths.
Curtis Nightstar let himself be pulled
because two hours of sleep
could not outrun Anri Sonohara or Rio Kamichika at the same time.
Anri had an idea why his body had given up on the chair.
A night. A search. A name she was not supposed to say in a hallway. I found Mika. Not the company. Not the reconstruction. Enough.
Rio did not have that piece. She had a desk-fall, a wanderer, a boy who collected other people’s gravity and called it oh dear.
Curtis peeled off toward the vending row like the girls had loaned him to the floor.
He bowed at the machine. Two coins. A can thunked down with religious timing.
“Canned coffee is a blessing.”
He cracked it. Bitter. Perfect. The first honest thing in his mouth since Namie’s two-sugar peace offering.
Rio folded her arms.
“That’s not lunch.”
“It’s architecture.” He toasted her with the can. “Holds the building up.”
Anri watched the way his hand shook once and then decided not to. She did not say Mika. She did not say you didn’t come home to sleep. She said, carefully:
“Sit. Please. You can drink that sitting.”
Curtis smiled at her like she had caught him being a person.
“Yes, Anri-chan.”
Rio’s eyes narrowed at the chan, then at Anri’s face, then at the can.
“If this is about last night,” Rio said, not knowing the map, “you could have texted that you were alive.”
“I was alive,” Curtis said. “I was also horizontal in the wrong building.”
Anri’s fingers tightened on her bag strap. Wrong building. Right girl. She looked at the floor tiles so Rio would not read her.
Curtis drank. The hallway fluorescent lights hummed.
On a vending-machine altar, canned coffee did its job.
Anri Sonohara carried a secret that explained the fall.
Rio Kamichika carried irritation
that did not have the right name yet.
Curtis Nightstar blessed a can
and did not give either of them the rest of the night.
Rio tipped her head.
“So did you go to a club?”
Curtis snorted into the can.
“I haven’t been to a club since L.A.” He leaned his shoulder on the machine like the metal might hold him up. “No. I was out wandering. Ended up on the edge of the city. Missed the train again, so I went to a hotel.”
Rio accepted it the way people accept weather. Annoyed. Possible.
“A hotel. You have a flat.”
“A flat is across a train. A hotel is a door that doesn’t ask questions at two in the morning.” He toasted her again. “Tourist logic.”
Anri’s smile did not reach her eyes. She knew the edge of the city had not been a hotel. She knew the door that had not asked questions had a kettle and a first-year attached. She drank her own silence and let the lie sit between them like a third person.
“Next time,” Rio said, “text. I thought you threw your phone in a lake again.”
“New phone. Old habit.” Curtis’s grin went crooked. “I’ll send a stamp next time gravity wins.”
Anri, very quiet: “As long as you got some sleep. Even two hours.”
“Hotel pillows are liars,” he said. “But they try.”
On a vending-machine lunch, the cover story checked in.
Rio Kamichika filed hotel.
Anri Sonohara filed he’s protecting someone again.
Curtis Nightstar drank canned coffee
and let L.A. clubs
take the blame for a night
that had never been a lobby.
Curtis unzipped his bag, looked inside, and grimaced like the canvas had told on him.
“That’s right. I never went home.”
No spare shirt. No bento. Yesterday’s life still folded in the bottom.
He stood. Stretch pulled his uniform tight across his shoulders. A couple of passing students forgot how hallways worked.
“Well. It can’t be helped.” Another pull of the can. Empty. “Coffee for lunch was good. Time for a well-deserved smoke.”
Rio stared at the bag.
“You came to class with last night still in there.”
“I’m consistent.”
Anri’s voice stayed small. “You could have come by. Or texted. I would have—” She stopped. Hotel. Edge of the city. The sentence had nowhere legal to go.
Curtis tipped two fingers at them, already angling for the stairwell that led to air.
“I will be on a roof being a bad influence. Do not follow if the teacher is looking. Do follow if I fall out of another chair.”
He went.
The bag hung off one shoulder like a confession he had not meant to unzip.
On a Raira lunch hour, home stayed unvisited.
Rio Kamichika watched a boy who packed a night and called it wandering.
Anri Sonohara watched the grimace
and knew the hotel
had never had a minibar.
Curtis Nightstar went to smoke on two hours, a lie,
and a bag that still thought it was yesterday.
Rio watched the stairwell eat him, then glanced at Anri’s face.
“Don’t feel so bad. I didn’t pack extra food either.” A shrug. Dry. Almost kind. “Would he even accept food?”
Anri’s fingers worried the strap of her bag.
“…He would say he’s fine. Then he would drink coffee and fall out of a desk.” She looked at the vending machine like it had failed a person. “He accepts help when it doesn’t look like help. A can is allowed. A bento might feel like someone noticed.”
Rio huffed.
“That’s stupid.”
“Yes.”
They stood in the leftover space of his stretch. Rio added, quieter:
“Hotel or not. He didn’t go home. That’s the part that matches.” She did not look at Anri when she said it. “If you were going to feel guilty, feel guilty about the staring yesterday. Not about a sandwich he would have refused.”
Anri’s mouth tightened. Then nodded once.
“I’m still going to bring him something tomorrow.”
Rio’s smile was small and unwilling.
“Then I’ll bring the drink. He can reject both of us evenly.”
On a hallway that still smelled like canned coffee,
two girls agreed he would not eat
if eating meant being seen.
Rio Kamichika asked the useful question.
Anri Sonohara already knew the answer
and packed the guilt anyway.
A shoulder hit Anri hard enough to turn her.
“Watch it!”
The girl did not stop to see who she had hit. Makeup. Laugh already aimed at a friend down the hall.
Rio stepped into the space like a door.
“You watch it. Pig.”
The girl scoffed, looked Rio up and down, found no audience worth a second round, and left with her chin high.
Rio exhaled through her nose. Then her voice dropped the blade.
“C’mon, Anri-chan. Let’s go eat.”
Anri blinked at the chan. At the snap. At the empty air where a collision had been.
“…You didn’t have to.”
“I did. People who walk through other people don’t get a free hallway.” Rio jerked her head toward the courtyard. “He went to smoke. We go eat. If you stand here feeling bad about a can of coffee, you’ll skip lunch too, and then there will be three disasters.”
Anri’s mouth almost smiled.
“Okay.”
They walked. Not quite side by side like friends from middle school. Close enough that the next shoulder would hit Rio first.
On a Raira hallway, a bump found a wall.
Rio Kamichika called Anri chan
and a stranger a pig
in the same minute.
Anri Sonohara followed
with her bag strap a little looser
than it had been
when Curtis disappeared up the stairs.
Rio said it to the courtyard stones, not quite looking at her.
“Curtis would have done the same for us. If he would for you. Then I don’t see why I can’t.”
Anri’s steps slowed.
The sentence had his name in it twice. Protection as a standard. Rio measuring herself against a boy who pulled people out of teachers and streets and desks.
“…He would,” Anri said. Soft. True. “He doesn’t check if it’s convenient.”
Rio’s jaw worked.
“Then I’m not going to stand there and watch someone walk through you because I’m not six-three and I don’t fall out of chairs for a living.” A beat. “Don’t make it a contest. I’m just saying the rule exists. He set it. I’m using it.”
Anri looked at her lunch bag. At the place Curtis had been.
“Thank you, Rio-chan.”
Rio clicked her tongue, ears a little warm.
“Eat. If you thank me again I’ll call you a pig too.”
On a courtyard path, protection copied itself.
Rio Kamichika borrowed Curtis Nightstar’s rule
and applied it to Anri Sonohara
without asking him for permission.
Anri heard if he would for you
and carried two truths at once:
he would —
and last night
he had already done more
than either of them were allowed to say.
The roof was wind and a cheap lighter.
Curtis had the cigarette halfway to his mouth when the phone went. He answered on the private channel before the ringtone finished being a problem.
“Curtis? Where are you?”
“I’m at school, Celty. Something wrong?”
“The apartment. No one’s home. It’s time.”
Curtis stared at the skyline. The can of coffee in his other hand suddenly felt like a joke.
Shit. That’s right. The sample.
Couch. Chair. Floor. Desk. Photos she had taken. Photos they had erased. The kit still sitting in his kitchen like a science project that had been stood up. She had come to him in the night because the house had cleaned itself. Now the house was empty again, and empty meant a second chance at the thing soap had already stolen once.
He pinched the cigarette, eyes on the courtyard far below — Anri and Rio somewhere with lunch, Mikado somewhere lying with a smile, Namie somewhere hunting a girl who was drinking bad tea.
“You’re sure they’re both out.”
“Lights off. No kettle. Shooter would have felt him in the hall.” A tight beat in the link. “I can scrape what they missed if there is anything left in the grain. I cannot carry a kit and a scythe and pretend this is a courier stop. You said coffee-with-cream was a comparison. I need the comparison while the rooms are honest.”
Curtis dragged, exhaled, watched the smoke go the wrong color in daylight.
“I’m in uniform. I fell out of a desk an hour ago. If I walk out of Raira now, two girls and a blond will invent a story.”
“Then invent a better one.” Not cruel. Urgent. “They cleaned last time because they came home. I will not get this window twice.”
He looked at the stairwell door. At the cigarette. At a night that had already spent him on Mika and Namie and two hours of sleep.
“…Don’t start without me. If you pull a floorboard and they walk in, you’re the one living there.” A breath. “Give me twenty. I’ll come. Kit’s at my place. I’ll grab it on the way.”
Celty’s smoke, even across the city, felt like a nod.
“Twenty. If a light comes on before you do, I leave. I will not get caught playing detective in my own kitchen.”
The line stayed open half a second longer. Then it was just roof wind and a boy who had promised a dullahan evidence
on a lunch break
that was no longer lunch.
Curtis killed the cigarette under his heel.
“Oh dear,” he told the empty air, not laughing this time.
On a Raira roof, the sample clock started.
Celty Sturluson had an empty apartment
and no one home.
Curtis Nightstar had twenty minutes,
a kit,
and a school that still thought
he was only tired.
Curtis looked around the empty roof.
“It can’t be helped.”
White mist gathered at his shoes first, then climbed — not Celty’s black, not a show. A screen. Enough that a window across the courtyard would only catch weather.
He bent his knees and launched.
The roof left him. Concrete cracked a complaint he did not stay to hear. The mist swallowed the arc. He hit an alley two streets over in a crouch that would have ruined a normal ankle, already running before the echo finished.
Back roads. Service gaps. The kind of Ikebukuro that did not put students on posters. His uniform became a dark streak. Braids snapped behind him. Two hours of sleep tried to collect a fee and got ignored.
He popped an AirPod Pro in on the move and called her without slowing.
“Celty? I’m on my way home. How much time do I got left?”
The link cut in over the slap of his shoes.
“You said twenty. You have used four.” A pause, sharper. “I am outside my own door like a thief. No kettle. No voices. If you are coming from Raira on a train you will miss it.”
“Not a train.” Breath even. Too even for the speed. “Kit first. Then you. Don’t go in yet if you can wait. If you can’t, photograph before you touch.”
“Curtis.” His name, not a lecture. “You sound like you are running.”
“I am a tourist.” He cut a corner hard enough that a stray cat chose a different life. “Tourists jog.”
Celty did not buy it. She also did not waste the window arguing physics.
“Twelve minutes before I stop trusting the quiet. After that I either scrape or I leave. I will not be standing in Shinra’s office when a key turns.”
“Copy.” The AirPod sat tight. The mist thinned off his heels so he would not look like a ghost on a main crosswalk. “Door’s going to slam at my place. Don’t spook. That’s me.”
On the back roads of Ikebukuro, a Raira uniform
became a deadline. Celty Sturluson held an empty apartment and a count.
Curtis Nightstar ran for a kit
like the sample had just grown teeth.
The flat ate thirty seconds.
Uniform hit the floor. White mist shoved the mess into a closet like it had been trained. He pulled the other kit off a hanger — the one that was not a costume if you ignored the history.
Black lab coat. Real fabric. Stolen once from a real building, then ruined on purpose: a red-and-white umbrella pressed into the breast pocket for a convention Justice had talked him into. If we’re going to look like scientists, we might as well look like the wrong ones. The symbol sat there now in Ikebukuro daylight, a joke with a dead brother’s fingerprints still in the stitch.
Black shirt. Red tie. White suspenders. White pants. Black belt. Black medical mask. Plain black cap pulled low. Braids out. Green eye. Blue eye.
Keys. Kit. Door.
The bike took the rest of the minutes personally.
Celty’s text hit the visor edge.
you got 10 more minutes.
Shinra just texted saying hes running late.
He doesnt know im home but told me anyways
Curtis parked three blocks short of the Kishitani building and ran the last of it on foot so the engine would not announce a guest. Stairs. Hall. The door she had cracked.
He was already inside when the count finished landing.
Breathing steady. Not a student. Not a tourist. The coat made him look like someone who belonged next to slides and bad decisions.
“How much time do we got?”
Celty stood in her own entry like a thief. Yellow helmet off. Smoke tight. She took him in — mask, cap, umbrella on the pocket, kit in his hand — and the private channel arrived before the PDA.
You changed.
Then, useful:
“Eight if his late is honest. Five if Mei is with him and she decides to be early.” She nodded toward the office. “Couch first. They cleaned the obvious. Grain and seams next. If you smell coffee-with-cream, say it. If you smell the other thing, say that too. I cannot.”
Her gloved hand flexed once at the sight of the logo.
“And if Shinra asks later why a man in an umbrella coat was in his hallway, I am telling him you are a very committed cosplayer.”
Curtis’s eyes creased above the mask.
“Justice would have loved that review.”
He set the kit on the table that had already lied once.
On a clock with eight minutes left,
the sample finally had a body attached.
Celty Sturluson had an empty home
and a late text from Shinra.
Curtis Nightstar stood in stolen science
and asked for time like time had ever been on their side.
Curtis worked fast.
Swabs. Bags. The kit opened like it had been waiting since the photos. Couch seam. Chair edge. Floor grain by the desk where soap had been too proud of itself. What was left was thin. Not nothing.
Then a bag in the corner that did not belong to Celty’s riding gear.
Mei’s clothes. Folded wrong. Left like a guest who intended to come back.
His brows furrowed above the mask.
“Well. I did say I wanted to be a detective.”
He did the part of the job that made the job ugly. Sample from the underwear. Labeled. Sealed. No commentary. The umbrella on his coat watched like a bad joke.
He turned.
“I hate to ask this. Where’s Shinra’s dirty clothes?”
Celty’s smoke jerked once.
The private channel came through tight, not shy — offended by the necessity.
You are standing in my house in a plague-doctor hat asking me for his laundry.
Then she pointed.
Hamper. Office side. The one Shinra always missed because he believed a wipe counted as a life.
“There. Don’t mix the bags.” A shorter beat. “If you find the same thing on both, I want the sentence in plain language when we are out of this room. Not later. Not kind.”
Curtis nodded once and moved.
Eight minutes had become six in the way minutes did when a key could turn.
On a floor that had already been cleaned once, evidence went into plastic.
Celty Sturluson pointed at a hamper like it was a crime scene.
Curtis Nightstar hated the question and asked it anyway —
because coffee-with-cream would not be enough if the underwear told the rest.